+## alpha2.2 graceful-tail hotfix
+
+alpha2.2 fixes the large-download truncation found in the live alpha2.1 test. **Both endpoints must be upgraded together.** The SMP3 hello version byte is bumped from 3 to 4 because alpha2.2 adds a logical `CLOSE` control frame; alpha2/alpha2.1 peers will reject the new hello instead of silently mixing incompatible close semantics. The JSON configuration schema is unchanged.
+
+- Removed alpha2/alpha2.1's fixed graceful-drain deadline (capped at 5 seconds). A closing logical stream now waits as long as cumulative ACKs keep making progress. `recovery_timeout` is used as a **no-progress/stall timeout**, not as an absolute tail-length limit.
+- Added a logical `CLOSE` control frame, broadcast over surviving legs after all locally accepted DATA has been cumulatively ACKed. This gives the peer an explicit logical EOF instead of forcing it to infer connection completion from carrier EOF and start an unnecessary reconnect cycle.
+- A routed/server-side EOF now enters graceful drain instead of immediately calling the fatal `fail()` path. The server session remains registered until the core actually finishes, so a leg that needs repair while draining can rejoin the same logical state.
+- During final CLOSE teardown, carrier read/write EOFs no longer schedule another recovery cycle. During the drain itself, normal leg recovery remains enabled.
+- Added regression coverage for a tail that takes longer than one recovery window in total while still making ACK progress, plus prompt peer shutdown through the logical CLOSE frame.
+
+### Why alpha2.1 could stop around the end of a large response
+
+The sender can have up to `max_inflight_frames * chunk_size` bytes accepted from the routed application but not yet cumulatively ACKed by the peer. With the default `1024 * 64KiB`, that can be roughly 64 MiB. alpha2.1 waited at most 5 seconds after local EOF and then tore down both carriers even if ACKs were still progressing. On a slower path this could truncate the tail of an otherwise healthy 256 MiB download. alpha2.2 waits for progress instead of enforcing that absolute 5-second cap.
+
